2. Collision Report

2.1 Methodological note

The collision search prioritizes work that would kill the novelty claim over work that would support it. A single strong collision is sufficient to force a narrowing; the absence of a collision is not evidence of novelty. References are treated as hypotheses of collision, not as established facts.

2.2 Collision matrix

Candidate Primary collision Collision level Disposition
C1 no-anticipation, conditional exchangeability, ignorability High Refined as conditional non-retroactivity
C2 provenance semirings, W3C PROV-O Moderate Reformulated as provenance triple
C3 partial identification, identifiability theory Near-total Absorbed into C4
C4 model-conditional attribution, Possibility Structure Theory High Retained as measure-transformation attribution

2.3 C1 — conditional non-retroactivity

The v0.1 formulation stated that any reverse edge from the realized outcome Y to the reconstructed past possibility space \mathcal{P}_t^{\text{past}} is a violation. This is too strong. Y can legitimately inform \mathcal{P}_t^{\text{past}} when the observation model O is independently justified. The correct rule is:

\boxed{
Y \to \mathcal{P}_t^{\text{past}} \text{ is not automatically invalid;}
\quad
Y \to \text{necessity of the realized past requires independent identification.}
}

Formally, the inference chain is conditioned on (m, H, O), and

A_i(\Delta \mathcal{P} \mid \tau_i, m, H, D_{\leq t}, O) \quad \text{not} \quad A_i(\Delta \mathcal{P} \mid \tau_i, m, H, Y).

Y may enter the inference only through O, and only if O can be specified from (D_{\leq t}, m, H) alone. If O is not independently justified, the inference is flagged as unidentified retroactive inference, not as a categorical violation.

2.4 C2 — provenance triple

The algebraic claim of the earlier draft — that provenance elements form a semiring via a product with an interaction term — is withdrawn. Adding an interaction term to a product of semirings does not automatically yield a semiring; associativity, commutativity, distributivity, identities, and closure must be demonstrated, and they have not been. We replace the semiring claim with a data structure:

\operatorname{Prov}(q) = (D_q, H_q, \Pi_q),

where D_q is the set of documents supporting q, H_q is the set of model hypotheses required for q, and \Pi_q \subseteq D_q \times H_q is a set of structural dependencies. No algebraic structure is claimed. If algebraic properties are later demonstrated, the triple can be promoted; until then, it remains a diagnostic object.

2.5 C3 — absorbed into C4

The claim that identifiability is a first-class output collides near-totally with partial identification and identifiability theory. The compatibility set

\Theta_i^\star = \{\theta \in \mathcal{S} : \mathcal{R}_m(D_i) \in \mathcal{I}^{-1}(\theta) \text{ and } \theta \text{ satisfies } H\}

is isomorphic to a partial-identification feasible set when \mathcal{S} is a normed space. C3 is therefore not retained as an independent contribution. It is absorbed into C4 as the identifiability dimension of the attribution status.

2.6 C4 — measure-transformation attribution

The claim that attribution is inference under (m, H) collides with model-conditional attribution in climate science and conditional attribution in causal inference. A candidate narrowing survives: the attribution object in HPTR is not the change in a result distribution but the measure transformation of an action-possibility set,

F_j : (\mathcal{A}_t, \boldsymbol{\mu}_t) \to (\mathcal{A}_{t+1}, \boldsymbol{\mu}_{t+1}),

with modes

\Delta \mathcal{A}^+ = \mathcal{A}_{t+1} \setminus \mathcal{A}_t, \quad
\Delta \mathcal{A}^- = \mathcal{A}_t \setminus \mathcal{A}_{t+1}, \quad
\Delta \boldsymbol{\mu} = \boldsymbol{\mu}_{t+1} - \boldsymbol{\mu}_t.

No existing framework formalizes the attribution object as a measure transformation on an action set. But the inferability of \Delta \boldsymbol{\mu} from documentary data is undemonstrated. C4 is retained in narrowed form with utility status undemonstrated.

