3. Epistemic Axioms

A1 — Primacy of the observed. Every claim about a human trajectory is traceable to a documented dataset D_i with sources, dates, and provenance.

A2 — Separation of levels. Observed, inferred, and abstract are never conflated. Each level carries a distinct epistemic status.

A3 — Identifiability bound. The level of formalization never exceeds the level of identifiability supplied by the data.

A4 — Explicit indeterminacy. When data do not decide between hypotheses, the system returns indeterminate and specifies the evidence that would reduce the indeterminacy.

A5 — Auditability. Every inference carries its sources S, its model m, its observation model O, and its uncertainty U. The footprint of any claim is

\text{Footprint} = (\Theta, U, S, m, O).

A6 — Non-retroactivity of the possible. The observation of a realized trajectory does not, by itself, constitute evidence that alternative trajectories were impossible, improbable, or inferior:

\text{realization} \nRightarrow \text{necessity},


\text{absence of evidence} \neq \text{evidence of absence},


\text{logical possibility} \neq \text{established historical possibility}.

A7 — Refutability. Any claim must be able to be contradicted by data. Non-refutable claims do not belong to the system.

A8 — Primacy of the trajectory. The primary object of conservation is the documented human trajectory \tau_i. The attributed transformation of possibilities F_i is a derived object, always accompanied by uncertainty. A documented trajectory with no identifiable transformation is retained.

A9 — Epistemic separation principle. The following are not equivalent:

\text{mathematically defined} \neq \text{empirically measurable} \neq \text{historically identifiable}.

Any claim about an object must declare its status on all three axes.

