4. Architecture

4.1 Five levels

Level Object Status Notation
0 Evidence Documented fact D_i
1 Observable events Structured observation E_i = \{e_1, \ldots, e_n\}
2 Reconstructed trajectory Model \tau_i = \mathcal{R}_m(D_i)
3 Dynamic signature Model of model \Theta_i = \mathcal{I}_m(\tau_i)
4 Attributed transformation Model-conditional inference A_i(\Delta \mathcal{P} \mid \tau_i, m, H, O)

The inference chain

D_i \longrightarrow E_i \longrightarrow \tau_i \longrightarrow \Theta_i \longrightarrow \Delta \mathcal{P}_i \longrightarrow A_i

carries an epistemic status at each arrow. A model of a model of a model is not three times truer; it is three times more fragile. Traceability is arrow-wise:

A_i \prec \Theta_i \prec \tau_i \prec E_i \prec D_i,

where \prec denotes epistemic dependence.

4.2 Conditional non-retroactivity

Let D_{\leq t} be the subset of documents temporally bounded by t. The reconstructed past possibility space is

\mathcal{P}_t^{\text{past}} = \mathcal{R}_m(D_{\leq t}, H),

and the attribution step is conditioned on (m, H, O), never on Y directly. Any inference that conditions on Y without an independently justified O is flagged as unidentified retroactive inference.

4.3 Provenance triple

For each query q in the inference chain,

\operatorname{Prov}(q) = (D_q, H_q, \Pi_q),

with hypothesis footprint

\operatorname{Hyp}(q) := H_q.

4.4 Attribution status

Attribution is characterized by a structured evidence profile

A_i = (A_M, A_T, A_N, A_D, A_C),

where each component is a line of evidence — absent, weak, moderate, or strong: A_M mechanism documented; A_T temporal precedence and non-substitution; A_N natural comparison; A_D dose–response; A_C theoretical coherence with an independently justified model. The output status is

\operatorname{Status}(F_i, \tau_i \mid m, H) \in \{\text{identified}, \text{partially identified}, \text{not identified}\},

with an explicit decision rule declared as part of (m, H).

4.5 Compatibility set and useful narrowing

The compatibility set is

\Theta_i^\star = \{\theta \in \mathcal{S} : \mathcal{R}_m(D_i) \in \mathcal{I}^{-1}(\theta) \text{ and } \theta \text{ satisfies } H\}.

Narrower is not automatically better. The criterion is

\boxed{\text{Useful narrowing} = \text{narrower} + \text{correct coverage} + \text{explicit additional assumptions}.}

