5. Mathematical Foundations

5.1 State space as measurable partition

Let (\mathcal{M}, \mathcal{B}, \nu) be a σ-finite measure space. A measurable partition of \mathcal{M} is a family \{\mathcal{M}_\sigma\}_{\sigma \in \Sigma} such that

\mathcal{M} = \bigsqcup_{\sigma \in \Sigma} \mathcal{M}_\sigma, \qquad \mathcal{M}_\sigma \in \mathcal{B}.

Whitney stratification is not assumed. Promotion of a measurable partition to a Whitney stratification is a modeling hypothesis m, not a structural assumption of HPTR.

5.2 Admissible action classes

Instantaneous movement of an agent at time t is represented not as a tangent vector — which would require a smooth structure not assumed in §5.1 — but as an equivalence class of admissible curves:

M(t) \in \Gamma_{\tau(t)} \mathcal{M} / \sim,

where \Gamma_{\tau(t)} \mathcal{M} is the set of admissible curves through \tau(t) and \sim is first-order contact. The relation \sim is an equivalence relation: reflexivity is trivial, symmetry follows from the symmetric role of the two curves, and transitivity follows from matching strata and derivatives near 0.

5.3 Trajectories

A trajectory is a piecewise-continuous map

\tau_i : [0, T_i] \to \mathcal{M}, \qquad \tau_i = \mathcal{R}_m(D_i).

The reconstruction operator \mathcal{R}_m depends on hypotheses of temporal granularity, interpolation, and source weighting.

5.4 Dynamic signatures

Let (\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{S}}) be a signature space. The signature operator is

\mathcal{I} : \operatorname{Paths}(\mathcal{M}) \to \mathcal{S}, \qquad \Theta_i = \mathcal{I}_m(\tau_i).

The family \mathcal{I} = \{\mathcal{I}_m\}_{m \in \mathcal{H}} is indexed by hypotheses. If \mathcal{I} is not injective, multiple trajectories can share the same signature. This is not a defect; it is the operational basis for identifiability analysis.

5.5 Factorized accessibility measures

Cost, salience, and legitimacy are semantically heterogeneous. We posit a vector of measures

\boldsymbol{\mu}_t = (\mu_t^{\mathrm{cost}}, \mu_t^{\mathrm{sal}}, \mu_t^{\mathrm{leg}}, \ldots),

with

\mu_t^{\mathrm{cost}} : \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_t) \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}, \quad
\mu_t^{\mathrm{sal}} : \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_t) \to [0, 1], \quad
\mu_t^{\mathrm{leg}} : \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_t) \to \Delta(\mathcal{L}).

The measured possibility space is

\mathcal{P}_t = (\mathcal{A}_t, \boldsymbol{\mu}_t).

Any dominance decision between actions must be explicit: partial order, Pareto dominance, or a declared aggregation.

5.6 Transformation of possibility

A transformation is a morphism of measured spaces:

F_j : (\mathcal{A}_t, \boldsymbol{\mu}_t) \to (\mathcal{A}_{t+1}, \boldsymbol{\mu}_{t+1}),

with three modes: opening \Delta \mathcal{A}^+, closing \Delta \mathcal{A}^-, and re-weighting \Delta \boldsymbol{\mu} on \mathcal{A}_t \cap \mathcal{A}_{t+1}. The re-weighting mode is the least formalized and remains an open problem.

Composition of transformations is associative by associativity of function composition applied componentwise.

5.7 Categorical layer — conjectural

The claim that transmissions form a category \mathbf{Trans} with cooperation as a colimit and rupture as anti-monotone is not justified by the definitions given. Identities and composition exist, and associativity holds, but the claimed correspondence between edge types and structural properties of morphisms is not established. The categorical layer is marked conjectural and is not used by the benchmark. If the benchmark succeeds, HPTR succeeds without category theory; category theory becomes optional mathematical enrichment rather than a hidden dependency.

