6. Identifiability, Indeterminacy, and Useful Narrowing

6.1 Compatibility set

Given D_i, m, H,

\Theta_i^\star = \{\theta \in \mathcal{S} : \mathcal{R}_m(D_i) \in \mathcal{I}^{-1}(\theta) \text{ and } \theta \text{ satisfies } H\}.

The system returns \Theta_i^\star itself. A derived width \rho_i = \operatorname{diam}_d(\Theta_i^\star) is reported only if a metric d on \mathcal{S} is justified; otherwise \rho_i is undefined and the raw compatibility set is retained.

6.2 Fundamental test

Given two trajectories \tau_A \neq \tau_B producing the same observable outcome Y_A = Y_B, if the system cannot distinguish them with D, it must return

\boxed{\text{indeterminate on } \Theta^\star}

and not invent a signature. This is where the system separates itself from a generative model.

6.3 Coverage and sharpness

For finite transformation space \mathcal{F} and estimator \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star,

\operatorname{Coverage} = P(F^{\text{true}} \in \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star), \qquad
\operatorname{Sharpness} = 1 - \frac{|\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star|}{|\mathcal{F}|}.

The trivial bounds are: if \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star = \mathcal{F}, then \operatorname{Coverage} = 1 and \operatorname{Sharpness} = 0; if |\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star| = 1 and \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star \neq \{F^{\text{true}}\}, then \operatorname{Coverage} = 0 and \operatorname{Sharpness} = 1 - 1/|\mathcal{F}|. Reducing |\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star| weakly decreases coverage and strictly increases sharpness.

6.4 Useful narrowing

An estimator \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_2 is a useful narrowing over \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_1 if

1. |\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_2| < |\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_1|;
2. \operatorname{Coverage}(\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_2) \geq \operatorname{Coverage}(\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_1) - \varepsilon for a declared \varepsilon \geq 0;
3. the assumptions added to obtain \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_2 are explicit in H.

Condition (3) is not automatic. A system can produce narrower sets by adding unstated assumptions, in which case the narrowing is not useful.

6.5 Indeterminacy as information

Indeterminacy is not failure. It is information on the limits of knowledge. Each indeterminate \Theta_i is accompanied by the evidence needed to reduce indeterminacy:

\mathcal{E}_i = \{\text{type of evidence that would reduce indeterminacy}\}.

The system states what it does not yet know and what would be required to know.

