7. Attribution

7.1 Rejection of scoring

Attribution is a matter of identifiability under assumptions, not of counting. The rule "3/5 implies attribution" is rejected. It is incompatible with modern causal inference.

7.2 Evidence dimensions

Attribution is characterized by a structured evidence profile

A_i = (A_M, A_T, A_N, A_D, A_C),

where each component is a line of evidence — absent, weak, moderate, or strong. None is aggregated into a score. The five dimensions are: mechanism documented; temporal precedence and non-substitution; natural comparison; dose–response; theoretical coherence.

7.3 Attribution status

The output is not a score but an epistemic status:

\operatorname{Status}(F_i, \tau_i \mid m, H) \in \{\text{identified}, \text{partially identified}, \text{not identified}\}.

Two models may legitimately produce different statuses. This is a property, not a bug.

7.4 Hypothesis diagnosis

For each query q,

H(q) = \{h : q \text{ changes materially when } h \text{ changes}\}.

The expected result of hypothesis diagnosis is not necessarily better estimation but better identification of which assumptions drive the conclusion.

---

8. Non-Retroactivity as Conditional Audit

8.1 Observation model

An observation model is a map

O : \mathcal{P}_t^{\text{past}} \to \Delta(\mathcal{Y}),

where O(y \mid \theta) denotes the likelihood of observing y given past possibility space \theta. O is independently justified if it can be specified from (D_{\leq t}, m, H) alone, without conditioning on Y.

8.2 Sound information flow

If O is independently justified, then conditioning on Y via Bayes' rule does not violate non-retroactivity. Let \pi(\theta \mid D_{\leq t}, m, H) be the prior over \mathcal{P}_t^{\text{past}}. Then

\pi(\theta \mid Y, D_{\leq t}, m, H, O) \propto O(Y \mid \theta) \cdot \pi(\theta \mid D_{\leq t}, m, H).

Since O is a function of (D_{\leq t}, m, H) only, no information is drawn from Y to determine the prior, and the update is sound.

8.3 Unidentified retroactive inference

If O is not independently justified, any inference from Y to \mathcal{P}_t^{\text{past}} requires at least one additional assumption not in (D_{\leq t}, m, H). Whether such an assumption is true is not determined by (D_{\leq t}, m, H). Hence the inference is not identified.

8.4 Twin indistinguishability

Let D_0, D_1 be observationally equivalent: every function measurable with respect to the observational data satisfies g(D_0) = g(D_1). Then any estimator f that is a function of observationally measurable data satisfies f(D_0) = f(D_1). If a system returns f(D_0) \neq f(D_1) for observationally equivalent D_0, D_1, then f uses information beyond the observational data, and the system produces false certainty. If F^{\text{true}}(D_0) \neq F^{\text{true}}(D_1) but the two trajectories are observationally equivalent, no observationally measurable system can recover F^{\text{true}}, and the correct output is the compatibility set containing both values.

