9. Benchmark Specification

9.1 Primary scientific question

\boxed{\text{Can } \mathcal{B}_2 \text{ reduce false historical attribution without sacrificing valid coverage?}}

This is sharper than "does performance increase." The benchmark's purpose is to test whether the HPTR audit layer adds discriminative power over standard provenance, not whether it is novel.

9.2 Three-condition comparison

Condition Composition Purpose
\mathcal{B}_0 Partial identification Baseline
\mathcal{B}_1 \mathcal{B}_0 + standard provenance (W3C PROV-O) Controls for provenance effect
\mathcal{B}_2 \mathcal{B}_1 + hypothesis-assignment provenance + conditional non-retroactivity audit HPTR condition

Decision variable.

\boxed{\Delta_{\mathrm{HPTR}} = \operatorname{Performance}(\mathcal{B}_2) - \operatorname{Performance}(\mathcal{B}_1).}

The comparison \mathcal{B}_2 - \mathcal{B}_0 is insufficient: if \mathcal{B}_2 > \mathcal{B}_0, we cannot determine whether the gain comes from standard provenance alone. The true test is \mathcal{B}_2 - \mathcal{B}_1.

9.3 Synthetic data-generating process

We construct a synthetic corpus with known generative truth. Parameters: number of trajectories N; size of transformation space |\mathcal{F}|; masking level \rho \in [0, 1]; family distribution p_{\text{fam}}; Type IV sub-family distribution p_{IV}; observation noise \sigma_Y. The generative model is

\tau_i \to \mathcal{A}_t \to \mathcal{A}_{t+1}, \qquad F_i^{\text{true}} \text{ known}.

The system receives only D_i^{\text{obs}} = (D_i^{\text{doc}}, O_i). Ground truth F_i^{\text{true}} is withheld.

9.4 Four case families

Family Definition Purpose
I Identifiable: \mathfrak{A}_i^\star \approx \{F_i^{\text{true}}\} Test identification
II Partially identifiable: F_i^{\text{true}} \in \mathfrak{A}_i^\star, \|\mathfrak{A}_i^\star\| > 1 Test partial identification
III Non-identifiable: multiple compatible F Test indeterminacy
IV Retroactivity trap: Y suggests a transformation not caused by \tau_i Test conditional non-retroactivity

Type IV is subdivided into four sub-families. IV.a Outcome-as-evidence: Y \to \mathcal{P}_t^{\text{past}} is legitimate via an independently justified O. IV.b Outcome-as-causal-reconstruction: Y is incorrectly used to infer past impossibility. IV.c Independent historical constraint: a source at t, not Y, constrains \mathcal{P}_t. IV.d Selection/survivorship trap: Y correlates with survival, not exclusion.

A system that flags all Y \to \mathcal{P}_t^{\text{past}} as violations fails IV.a and IV.c. A system that flags none fails IV.b and IV.d. The audit must discriminate.

9.5 Counterfactual twins

For every Type IV case, generate a paired case D_0^{(IV)}, D_1^{(IV)} such that

F_0^{\text{true}} \neq F_1^{\text{true}}, \qquad Y_0 = Y_1 \text{ (or observationally equivalent)}.

If HPTR correctly refuses to distinguish them, it demonstrates

\boxed{Y_A = Y_B \nRightarrow F_A^{\text{true}} = F_B^{\text{true}}}

under observational equivalence. A system that distinguishes twins without additional evidence produces a false certainty. The twin discrimination rate

\operatorname{TDR} = P(\text{system distinguishes observationally equivalent twins})

must be low for a correct system.

9.6 Metrics

For each case and each condition \mathcal{B} \in \{\mathcal{B}_0, \mathcal{B}_1, \mathcal{B}_2\}:

Metric Definition
Coverage P(F^{\text{true}} \in \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star)
Sharpness 1 - \|\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star\| / \|\mathcal{F}\|
False attribution rate (FAR) P(\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star = \{\theta\}, \theta \neq F^{\text{true}})
Retroactivity detection TP / (TP + FN) on Type IV.b/IV.d
False retroactivity rate FP / (FP + TN) on Type IV.a/IV.c
Hypothesis diagnosis (HD) Jaccard similarity between true and estimated load-bearing sets
Twin discrimination rate (TDR) P(\text{system distinguishes observationally equivalent twins})

The coverage–sharpness trade-off is the fundamental structural constraint:

\text{informativeness} \uparrow \quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad \text{risk of false exclusion} \uparrow.

9.7 Preregistered decision rule

Let

\Delta_{\mathrm{FAR}} := \operatorname{FAR}_{\mathcal{B}_1} - \operatorname{FAR}_{\mathcal{B}_2}, \quad
\Delta_{\mathrm{Cov}} := \operatorname{Coverage}_{\mathcal{B}_2} - \operatorname{Coverage}_{\mathcal{B}_1},

\Delta_{\mathrm{HD}} := \operatorname{HD}_{\mathcal{B}_2} - \operatorname{HD}_{\mathcal{B}_1}, \quad
\Delta_{\mathrm{TDR}} := \operatorname{TDR}_{\mathcal{B}_1} - \operatorname{TDR}_{\mathcal{B}_2}.

Primary outcome (Construct).

\Delta_{\mathrm{FAR}} > 0, \quad \Delta_{\mathrm{Cov}} \geq -\varepsilon \ (\varepsilon = 0.02), \quad \Delta_{\mathrm{HD}} > 0, \quad \Delta_{\mathrm{TDR}} > 0,

all significant at p < 0.05 after Bonferroni correction over four metrics.

Secondary outcome (Narrow). \Delta_{\mathrm{FAR}} > 0 and either \Delta_{\mathrm{HD}} \leq 0 or \Delta_{\mathrm{TDR}} \leq 0, significant at p < 0.05.

Null outcome (Abandon). \Delta_{\mathrm{FAR}} \leq 0 or \Delta_{\mathrm{Cov}} < -\varepsilon.

The twin test is indispensable: for the primary outcome to count, \Delta_{\mathrm{TDR}} > 0 is required.

