Appendix A — Mathematical Foundations

A.1 State space and measurable partition

Definition A.1 (State space). A state space is a σ-finite measure space (\mathcal{M}, \mathcal{B}, \nu), where \mathcal{M} is the set of human states, \mathcal{B} is its Borel σ-algebra, and \nu is a reference measure.

Definition A.2 (Measurable partition). A measurable partition of \mathcal{M} is a family \{\mathcal{M}_\sigma\}_{\sigma \in \Sigma} such that:

1. \mathcal{M} = \bigsqcup_{\sigma \in \Sigma} \mathcal{M}_\sigma (disjoint union);
2. \mathcal{M}_\sigma \in \mathcal{B} for each \sigma;
3. The index map \sigma \mapsto \mathcal{M}_\sigma is measurable with respect to a declared σ-algebra on \Sigma.

Remark A.1. Whitney stratification is not assumed. Promotion of a measurable partition to a Whitney stratification is a modeling hypothesis m, not a structural assumption of HPTR.

Remark A.2. The choice of σ-algebra on \Sigma is part of the modeling hypothesis m. If \Sigma is finite or countable, the discrete σ-algebra is a default.

A.2 Admissible curves and action classes

Definition A.3 (Admissible curve). A curve \gamma : [0, 1] \to \mathcal{M} is admissible from x \in \mathcal{M} if \gamma(0) = x, \gamma is piecewise C^1, and the piecewise segments remain within strata of the partition (i.e., \gamma([0, \varepsilon)) \subseteq \mathcal{M}_{\sigma(x)} for some \varepsilon > 0).

Definition A.4 (First-order contact). Two admissible curves \gamma_1, \gamma_2 from x are first-order equivalent, denoted \gamma_1 \sim \gamma_2, if there exists \varepsilon > 0 such that for all t \in [0, \varepsilon):

1. \gamma_1(t) and \gamma_2(t) lie in the same stratum \mathcal{M}_{\sigma(x)};
2. The directional derivatives agree to first order when defined.

Proposition A.1 (Equivalence relation). The relation \sim is an equivalence relation on admissible curves from x.

Proof.

· Reflexivity. \gamma \sim \gamma: the curve matches itself in strata and derivatives.
· Symmetry. If \gamma_1 \sim \gamma_2, the defining conditions are symmetric in (\gamma_1, \gamma_2), so \gamma_2 \sim \gamma_1.
· Transitivity. If \gamma_1 \sim \gamma_2 and \gamma_2 \sim \gamma_3, then all three curves lie in \mathcal{M}_{\sigma(x)} near 0 and have matching first-order derivatives. Hence \gamma_1 \sim \gamma_3.

\square

Definition A.5 (Action class). The set of admissible action classes at x is

M(x) := \{[\gamma]_\sim : \gamma \text{ admissible from } x\}.

Remark A.3. This replaces the tangent-space formulation of earlier drafts. The tangent space T_x \mathcal{M} is a special case when \mathcal{M} is a smooth manifold; the present formulation is strictly weaker and consistent with §5.1 of the main text.

Remark A.4. The decomposition \{\Delta x, \Delta s, \Delta g, \Delta i, \Delta c\} is a modeling assumption indexed by m, not an ontological claim. It is the choice of a basis for the action class; different m may yield different decompositions.

A.3 Trajectories and reconstruction

Definition A.6 (Trajectory). A trajectory of agent i over [0, T_i] is a piecewise-continuous map

\tau_i : [0, T_i] \to \mathcal{M}.

The set of trajectories is denoted \operatorname{Paths}(\mathcal{M}).

Definition A.7 (Reconstruction operator). For a model m \in \mathcal{H},

\mathcal{R}_m : \mathcal{D} \to \operatorname{Paths}(\mathcal{M}), \qquad \tau_i = \mathcal{R}_m(D_i).

Remark A.5. \mathcal{R}_m is a modeling object, not an empirical one. Its choice is part of the footprint. Different m may yield different \tau_i from the same D_i, and this divergence is itself informative.

Proposition A.2 (Reconstruction is not canonical). There is no canonical \mathcal{R}_m independent of m.

Proof. Suppose a canonical \mathcal{R} existed. Then for all datasets D and all models m, \mathcal{R}(D) = \mathcal{R}_m(D). But reconstruction requires choices of temporal granularity, interpolation, and source weighting, all of which are model-dependent. A choice of these is a choice of m; hence no canonical \mathcal{R} exists. \square

A.4 Signature operator

Definition A.8 (Signature space). A signature space is a pair (\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{S}}) where \mathcal{S} is a set and \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{S}} is a σ-algebra.

Definition A.9 (Signature operator). The signature operator is

\mathcal{I} : \operatorname{Paths}(\mathcal{M}) \to \mathcal{S}, \qquad \Theta_i = \mathcal{I}_m(\tau_i).

The family \mathcal{I} = \{\mathcal{I}_m\}_{m \in \mathcal{H}} is indexed by hypotheses.

Proposition A.3 (Signature non-injectivity). If \mathcal{I} is not injective, multiple trajectories can share the same signature.

Proof. If \mathcal{I}(\tau_1) = \mathcal{I}(\tau_2) with \tau_1 \neq \tau_2, then any inference from \mathcal{I}(\tau) alone cannot distinguish \tau_1 from \tau_2. The compatibility set \Theta_i^\star therefore contains \mathcal{I}(\tau_1) = \mathcal{I}(\tau_2). \square

Remark A.6. Non-injectivity is not a defect; it is the operational basis for identifiability analysis. A signature operator that was injective on all of \operatorname{Paths}(\mathcal{M}) would make identifiability trivial and would render the compatibility-set formalism unnecessary.

Definition A.10 (Compatibility set). Given D_i, m, H,

\Theta_i^\star = \{\theta \in \mathcal{S} : \mathcal{R}_m(D_i) \in \mathcal{I}^{-1}(\theta) \text{ and } \theta \text{ satisfies } H\}.

A.5 Factorized accessibility measures

Definition A.11 (Accessibility vector). At time t,

\boldsymbol{\mu}_t = (\mu_t^{\mathrm{cost}}, \mu_t^{\mathrm{sal}}, \mu_t^{\mathrm{leg}}, \ldots)

with

\mu_t^{\mathrm{cost}} : \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_t) \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}, \quad
\mu_t^{\mathrm{sal}} : \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_t) \to [0, 1], \quad
\mu_t^{\mathrm{leg}} : \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_t) \to \Delta(\mathcal{L}).

Definition A.12 (Measured possibility space). \mathcal{P}_t = (\mathcal{A}_t, \boldsymbol{\mu}_t).

Remark A.7. The components of \boldsymbol{\mu}_t are semantically heterogeneous and cannot be added without an explicit aggregation. This is why we do not posit a single scalar measure.

A.6 Transformations

Definition A.13 (Transformation). A transformation is a pair (\varphi_A, \varphi_{\boldsymbol{\mu}}) such that

F = (\varphi_A, \varphi_{\boldsymbol{\mu}}) : (\mathcal{A}_t, \boldsymbol{\mu}_t) \to (\mathcal{A}_{t+1}, \boldsymbol{\mu}_{t+1}),

where \varphi_A : \mathcal{A}_t \to \mathcal{A}_{t+1} is measurable and \varphi_{\boldsymbol{\mu}} is a family of measurable maps \mu_t^c \to \mu_{t+1}^c for each component c \in \{\mathrm{cost}, \mathrm{sal}, \mathrm{leg}, \ldots\}.

Definition A.14 (Modes).

\Delta \mathcal{A}^+ := \mathcal{A}_{t+1} \setminus \varphi_A(\mathcal{A}_t) \quad \text{(opening)},

\Delta \mathcal{A}^- := \mathcal{A}_t \setminus \varphi_A^{-1}(\mathcal{A}_{t+1}) \quad \text{(closing)},

\Delta \boldsymbol{\mu} := \boldsymbol{\mu}_{t+1} - \boldsymbol{\mu}_t \quad \text{on } \mathcal{A}_t \cap \mathcal{A}_{t+1} \quad \text{(re-weighting)}.

Proposition A.4 (Composition of transformations). The composition of two transformations

F_1 : (\mathcal{A}_t, \boldsymbol{\mu}_t) \to (\mathcal{A}_{t+1}, \boldsymbol{\mu}_{t+1}), \quad
F_2 : (\mathcal{A}_{t+1}, \boldsymbol{\mu}_{t+1}) \to (\mathcal{A}_{t+2}, \boldsymbol{\mu}_{t+2})

is a transformation

F_2 \circ F_1 : (\mathcal{A}_t, \boldsymbol{\mu}_t) \to (\mathcal{A}_{t+2}, \boldsymbol{\mu}_{t+2}).

Proof. Define \varphi_A^{21} := \varphi_A^2 \circ \varphi_A^1. Composition of measurable maps is measurable, so \varphi_A^{21} is measurable. Define \varphi_{\boldsymbol{\mu}}^{21} componentwise by \varphi_{\mu^c}^{21} := \varphi_{\mu^c}^2 \circ \varphi_{\mu^c}^1, again measurable. \square

Proposition A.5 (Associativity of composition). For transformations F_1, F_2, F_3,

(F_3 \circ F_2) \circ F_1 = F_3 \circ (F_2 \circ F_1).

Proof. Immediate from associativity of function composition, applied to each component. \square

Remark A.8. We do not claim that transformations form a category in the technical sense without first constructing identity morphisms and verifying closure under composition (see Appendix E).

