Appendix B — Non-Retroactivity: Formal Statement and Proofs

B.1 Observation model

Definition B.1 (Observation model). An observation model is a map

O : \mathcal{P}_t^{\text{past}} \to \Delta(\mathcal{Y}),

where \Delta(\mathcal{Y}) is the space of probability distributions on the outcome space \mathcal{Y}. O(y \mid \theta) denotes the likelihood of observing y given that the past possibility space was \theta.

Definition B.2 (Independent justification). An observation model O is independently justified if it can be specified from sources other than Y and without conditioning on Y. Equivalently, O is a function of (D_{\leq t}, m, H) only.

B.2 Conditional non-retroactivity

Rule B.1 (Conditional non-retroactivity). Let D_{\leq t} be documents temporally bounded by t. The attribution step is written

A_i(\Delta \mathcal{P} \mid \tau_i, m, H, D_{\leq t}, O),

not

A_i(\Delta \mathcal{P} \mid \tau_i, m, H, Y).

Y may enter the inference only through O, and only if O is independently justified.

Proposition B.1 (Soundness of legitimate information flow). If O is independently justified, then conditioning on Y via Bayes' rule does not violate non-retroactivity.

Proof. Let \pi(\theta \mid D_{\leq t}, m, H) be the prior over \mathcal{P}_t^{\text{past}}. Bayes' rule gives

\pi(\theta \mid Y, D_{\leq t}, m, H, O) \propto O(Y \mid \theta) \cdot \pi(\theta \mid D_{\leq t}, m, H).

Since O is a function of (D_{\leq t}, m, H) only, no information is drawn from Y to determine the prior. The likelihood O(Y \mid \theta) is specified before observing Y. Hence the update is sound and non-retroactive. \square

Proposition B.2 (Unidentified retroactive inference). If O is not independently justified, then any inference from Y to \mathcal{P}_t^{\text{past}} is not identified from (D_{\leq t}, m, H).

Proof. By hypothesis, O cannot be specified from (D_{\leq t}, m, H). Any inference that uses Y must therefore require at least one additional assumption not in (D_{\leq t}, m, H). Whether such an assumption is true is not determined by (D_{\leq t}, m, H). Hence the inference is not identified. \square

Corollary B.1. Any inference that conditions on Y without an independently justified O is flagged as unidentified retroactive inference.

B.3 Twin indistinguishability

Definition B.3 (Observational equivalence). Two datasets D_0, D_1 are observationally equivalent if every function g measurable with respect to the observational data satisfies g(D_0) = g(D_1).

Definition B.4 (Estimator). An estimator of F^{\text{true}} is a function f : \mathcal{D} \to \mathcal{F} measurable with respect to observational data.

Proposition B.3 (Twin indistinguishability). Let D_0, D_1 be observationally equivalent. Then any estimator f that is a function of observationally measurable data satisfies f(D_0) = f(D_1).

Proof. Suppose f(D_0) \neq f(D_1). Since f is a function of observational data, f itself is an observationally measurable distinguishing function, contradicting observational equivalence of D_0 and D_1. \square

Corollary B.2 (False certainty). If a system returns f(D_0) \neq f(D_1) for observationally equivalent D_0, D_1, then f uses information beyond the observational data. The system is producing false certainty.

Corollary B.3 (Sharp twin). If F^{\text{true}}(D_0) \neq F^{\text{true}}(D_1) but the two trajectories are observationally equivalent, then no observationally measurable system can recover F^{\text{true}}. The correct output is the compatibility set containing both values.

Remark B.1. Corollary B.3 is the mathematical basis for the Type IV benchmark family. The benchmark tests whether a system respects this bound.

