Appendix C — Provenance Triple: Structure and Non-Algebraicity

C.1 Definition

Definition C.1 (Provenance triple). For a query q in the inference chain, the provenance is

\operatorname{Prov}(q) = (D_q, H_q, \Pi_q),

where:

· D_q \subseteq \mathcal{D} is the set of documents supporting q;
· H_q \subseteq \mathcal{H} \cup \{m\} is the set of model hypotheses required for q;
· \Pi_q \subseteq D_q \times H_q is a set of structural dependencies.

Definition C.2 (Hypothesis footprint). \operatorname{Hyp}(q) := H_q.

Definition C.3 (Load-bearing hypothesis). A hypothesis h \in H_q is load-bearing for q if there exists a modification h' of h such that the output of q changes materially under h'.

C.2 Positive claims

Claim C.1 (Data structure). \operatorname{Prov}(q) is a well-defined data structure for any query q.

Proof. D_q and H_q are subsets of finite declared sets (assuming finite corpora and hypothesis sets, which holds for practical instances), and \Pi_q is a subset of their product. \square

Claim C.2 (Diagnostic value). \operatorname{Hyp}(q) identifies the set of hypotheses required for q, enabling load-bearing analysis.

Proof. By Definition C.3, load-bearing analysis is a well-defined operation on \operatorname{Prov}(q). \square

C.3 Negative claim: failure of the naive semiring structure

Proposition C.1 (Failure of the naive semiring claim). There is no natural semiring structure on D_q \times H_q inherited component-wise from semiring structures on D_q and H_q when an interaction term \iota is present.

Proof. Suppose the operation is defined by

(D_1, H_1) \cdot (D_2, H_2) = (D_1 D_2 + \iota(H_1, H_2), H_1 H_2).

Then distributivity would require

(D_1 + \iota_1(H), H) \cdot (D_2, H_2) = (D_1 D_2 + \iota_1(H) D_2, H H_2).

But the cross term \iota_1(H) D_2 is not naturally an element of D_q \times H_q, since \iota_1(H) is not a document. Additional structure would be required to close the algebra under multiplication. \square

Corollary C.1. The semiring claim of earlier drafts is withdrawn.

C.4 Open problem

Open Problem C.1. Under what conditions does the provenance triple admit a semiring structure? Sufficient conditions might include:

· restriction to a subset of queries;
· additional closure properties on D and H;
· a different algebraic structure (e.g., a monoid, a rig without distributivity).

Until this is resolved, no algebraic claim is admitted.

