Appendix D — Compatibility Sets and Useful Narrowing

D.1 Definitions

Definition D.1 (Compatibility set). Given D_i, m, H,

\Theta_i^\star = \{\theta \in \mathcal{S} : \mathcal{R}_m(D_i) \in \mathcal{I}^{-1}(\theta) \text{ and } \theta \text{ satisfies } H\}.

Definition D.2 (Coverage). For finite transformation space \mathcal{F} and estimator \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star,

\operatorname{Coverage} = P(F^{\text{true}} \in \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star).

Definition D.3 (Sharpness). For finite \mathcal{F},

\operatorname{Sharpness} = 1 - \frac{|\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star|}{|\mathcal{F}|}.

D.2 Trivial bounds

Proposition D.1 (Coverage–sharpness trade-off). For any estimator \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star \subseteq \mathcal{F}:

1. If \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star = \mathcal{F}, then \operatorname{Coverage} = 1 and \operatorname{Sharpness} = 0.
2. If |\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star| = 1 and \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star \neq \{F^{\text{true}}\}, then \operatorname{Coverage} = 0 and \operatorname{Sharpness} = 1 - 1/|\mathcal{F}|.
3. More generally, reducing |\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star| weakly decreases coverage and strictly increases sharpness.

Proof.

(i) If \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star = \mathcal{F}, then F^{\text{true}} \in \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star trivially, so \operatorname{Coverage} = 1. \operatorname{Sharpness} = 1 - |\mathcal{F}|/|\mathcal{F}| = 0.

(ii) If |\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star| = 1 and \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star = \{\theta\} with \theta \neq F^{\text{true}}, then F^{\text{true}} \notin \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star, so \operatorname{Coverage} = 0. \operatorname{Sharpness} = 1 - 1/|\mathcal{F}|.

(iii) Coverage is monotone non-decreasing in \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star (larger sets cover more). Sharpness is monotone strictly decreasing in |\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star|. Hence the trade-off. \square

Corollary D.1. A system achieving \operatorname{Coverage} = 1 with \operatorname{Sharpness} > 0 must have additional information about F^{\text{true}} beyond the model alone.

D.3 Useful narrowing

Definition D.4 (Useful narrowing). An estimator \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_2 is a useful narrowing over \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_1 if:

1. |\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_2| < |\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_1| (narrower);
2. \operatorname{Coverage}(\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_2) \geq \operatorname{Coverage}(\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_1) - \varepsilon for a declared \varepsilon \geq 0 (coverage maintained up to \varepsilon);
3. The assumptions added to obtain \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_2 are explicit in H.

Proposition D.2 (Non-tautology). Condition (3) is not automatic.

Proof. Consider a system that returns \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_2 = \{F^{\text{true}}\} by assuming F^{\text{true}} is known. Then |\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_2| = 1 < |\widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_1|, but condition (3) fails because the assumption is not explicit. \square

Remark D.1. The benchmark must therefore audit the assumption set accompanying each estimate, not only the estimate's size and coverage.

