Appendix E — Categorical Layer (Conjectural)

E.1 Statement of the conjecture

Conjecture E.1. The class of measured action spaces (\mathcal{A}, \boldsymbol{\mu}) and transformations F = (\varphi_A, \varphi_{\boldsymbol{\mu}}) forms a category \mathbf{Trans} in which:

· Objects are measured action spaces;
· Morphisms are measurable transformations preserving or transforming \boldsymbol{\mu};
· Composition is ordinary composition of maps;
· Identity morphisms are identity transformations.

Conjecture E.2. The five edge types are structural properties of morphisms in \mathbf{Trans}:

Type Proposed structural property
Inheritance \boldsymbol{\mu}-monotone
Rupture \boldsymbol{\mu}-anti-monotone
Influence \boldsymbol{\mu}-bounded non-monotone
Cooperation Colimit of a diagram in \mathbf{Trans}
Convergence No morphism; metric similarity of signatures

E.2 What is proven

Proposition E.1 (Partial result). Identities and composition exist; associativity holds.

Proof.

· Identities. The identity map \operatorname{id}_{(\mathcal{A}, \boldsymbol{\mu})} preserves \boldsymbol{\mu} and is measurable.
· Composition. Composition of measurable maps is measurable, and preservation/transformation of \boldsymbol{\mu} is preserved under composition if the transformation types are compatible.
· Associativity. By Proposition A.5 applied componentwise. \square

Proposition E.2 (Failure of cooperation–colimit claim). The claimed correspondence "cooperation \leftrightarrow colimit" is not established by the definitions.

Proof. A colimit requires a diagram of objects and morphisms. In \mathbf{Trans}, the only morphisms defined are single-trajectory transformations. Joint action by two agents i, j is not, in general, expressible as a colimit of individual transformations without specifying a monoidal structure or a more general 2-categorical framework. \square

E.3 Exclusion from benchmark

Policy E.1. The benchmark is independent of the categorical layer. All metrics, case families, and estimators are defined without reference to \mathbf{Trans}. If the benchmark succeeds, the conceptual core of HPTR succeeds without category theory.

Rationale. This policy ensures that success or failure of the benchmark is not confounded by an unproven mathematical structure.

