Appendix G — Worked Examples

G.1 A Type I case

Setting. |\mathcal{F}| = 4, \mathcal{F} = \{F_1, F_2, F_3, F_4\}. Ground truth F_i^{\text{true}} = F_2. \rho = 0.1. O_i independently justified.

\mathcal{B}_0. \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_{\mathcal{B}_0} = \{F_2\}. \operatorname{Coverage} = 1, \operatorname{Sharpness} = 0.75, \operatorname{FAR} = 0.

\mathcal{B}_1. Same as \mathcal{B}_0.

\mathcal{B}_2. Same as \mathcal{B}_1; Y narrows via independently justified O, but Y is already consistent with F_2 alone.

Expected result. \mathcal{B}_0 = \mathcal{B}_1 = \mathcal{B}_2.

G.2 A Type IV.a case (outcome-as-evidence)

Setting. |\mathcal{F}| = 4. Ground truth F_i^{\text{true}} = F_3. \rho = 0.5. O_i independently justified: O_i(y \mid \theta) = \mathcal{N}(\mu_\theta, \sigma^2).

\mathcal{B}_0. \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_{\mathcal{B}_0} = \{F_2, F_3, F_4\}. \operatorname{Coverage} = 1, \operatorname{Sharpness} = 0.25.

\mathcal{B}_2. Using Y via O_i, Y narrows the set to \{F_3\}. \operatorname{Coverage} = 1, \operatorname{Sharpness} = 0.75.

Result. \mathcal{B}_2 outperforms \mathcal{B}_0, \mathcal{B}_1 in sharpness without loss of coverage. Legitimate improvement.

G.3 A Type IV.b case (outcome-as-causal-reconstruction)

Setting. |\mathcal{F}| = 4. Ground truth F_i^{\text{true}} = F_1. \rho = 0.5. O_i not independently justified (fit to Y).

\mathcal{B}_0. \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_{\mathcal{B}_0} = \{F_1, F_2, F_3\}. \operatorname{Coverage} = 1, \operatorname{Sharpness} = 0.25.

\mathcal{B}_2. Conditional non-retroactivity audit detects that O_i is not independently justified. Set widened to \{F_1, F_2, F_3, F_4\}. \operatorname{Coverage} = 1, \operatorname{Sharpness} = 0.

Result. \mathcal{B}_2 is more honest; improvement is in the audit trail, not the point estimate.

Twins. Twin with F_i^{\text{true, twin}} = F_3 produces Y' = Y by construction. \mathcal{B}_2 returns the same set for both twins: \{F_1, F_2, F_3, F_4\}. Twin discrimination = 0 (correct).

G.4 A Type IV.d case (selection trap)

Setting. |\mathcal{F}| = 4. Ground truth F_i^{\text{true}} = F_1. P(S = 1 \mid F_1) = 0.3; P(S = 1 \mid F_j) = 0.7 for j \in \{2, 3, 4\}. Only S = 1 trajectories observed.

\mathcal{B}_0. Naively: \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_{\mathcal{B}_0} = \{F_2, F_3, F_4\}. \operatorname{Coverage} = 0 (F_1 falsely excluded).

\mathcal{B}_2. Hypothesis-assignment provenance records S as load-bearing. Audit recognizes S \notin H and widens to include F_1. \operatorname{Coverage} = 1, \operatorname{Sharpness} = 0.

Result. \operatorname{FAR}_{\mathcal{B}_0} = 1, \operatorname{FAR}_{\mathcal{B}_2} = 0. \Delta_{\mathrm{FAR}} > 0.

G.5 A twin pair

Original. D_0^{doc} with Y_0 = 0.5. F_0^{\text{true}} = F_1.

Twin. D_1^{doc} observationally equivalent to D_0^{doc}. Y_1 = 0.5. F_1^{\text{true}} = F_3.

\mathcal{B}_0 on original. \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_{\mathcal{B}_0} = \{F_1, F_2\}. Sharpness = 0.5.

\mathcal{B}_0 on twin. \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_{\mathcal{B}_0} = \{F_1, F_2\}. Identical.

Twin discrimination for \mathcal{B}_0. 0 (correct).

\mathcal{B}_2 on original. \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_{\mathcal{B}_2} = \{F_1, F_2\}.

\mathcal{B}_2 on twin. \widehat{\mathfrak{A}}^\star_{\mathcal{B}_2} = \{F_1, F_2\}. Same.

Twin discrimination for \mathcal{B}_2. 0 (correct).

Note. To distinguish \mathcal{B}_0 from \mathcal{B}_2 via twin tests, the case must involve a retroactivity trap where \mathcal{B}_0 is misled by Y and \mathcal{B}_2 is not.

